think r1 and r2 held the roster in memory. A dev-net a keeper cannot hand to another is only a local fixture; r3 lets the whole constellation cross as a format constel-v1 record (one ship <name> <pk-hex> line each), the family _bron travel idiom.
think The self-certifying insight is unique to Constel and free: since a ship's key is a pure function of its name (r1: seed = SHA-256(name)), the record's carried pk can be re-derived from the name at parse and checked — a tampered key refuses PkMismatch, so a key can never ride a real name. Richer than the puddle_bron analog, which carries opaque host keys.
think The crux is not merely byte round-trip but that a keeper boots the IDENTICAL constellation from the record alone — proven by reaching the byte-identical quorum head as the in-memory roster over a demo dag (and that head is the Dag's true order-head).
obs GREEN on the first build and run. Modeled the reader on puddle_bron (to_hex/from_hex/tokens, usize seam-only with // seam: comment); refusals cover BadFormat, UnknownField, and the PkMismatch tamper (flip the last hex nibble of the first ship line's key). Composes constel public API only; r1 re-runs GREEN at the door; demo seeds, no custody.
